  {
    "question": "What synthetic gene circuits, like the repressilator and genetic toggle switch, have been built and characterized in synthetic biology?",
    "answer": "In synthetic biology, several synthetic gene circuits have been built and characterized, including the repressilator and genetic toggle switch. The repressilator, constructed by Elowitz and Leibler, is a negative feedback loop consisting of three repressors that creates oscillatory behavior, functioning as the first engineered gene-network oscillator [5][14]. The genetic toggle switch, engineered by the Collins lab, is a bistable circuit that can switch between two stable states in response to external stimuli, effectively acting as a one-bit memory [5][11]. \n\nAdditionally, there are variations of the toggle switch, such as the CRISPRi mutual-repression toggle, which utilizes CRISPR interference for bistability, and an optogenetic bistable toggle that is controlled by light [12][13]. These circuits exemplify the advancements in synthetic biology, allowing for the design of complex genetic systems with specific functionalities.",
